n" [:option {:value "12"} "1948 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "13"} "1952 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "14"} "1956 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "15"} "1960 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "16"} "1964 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "17"} "1968 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "19"} "1976 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "20"} "1980 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "21"} "1984 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "22"} "1988 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "23"} "1992 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "24"} "1996 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "25"} "2000 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "26"} "2004 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "53"} "2008 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "65"} "2010 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "54"} "2012 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "67"} "2014 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "59"} "2016 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "69"} "2018 Summer Youth Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "61"} "2020 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:option {:value "63"} "2024 Summer Olympics"]]] [:script {} "$('#sport_in_other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/editions/' + $('#sport_in_other_games_select').val() + '/sports/SWM'})"] "\n" [:h1 {} "Swimming at the 1972 Summer Olympics"] "\n" [:table {:class "biodata"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Dates"] "\n" [:td {} "28 August –  4 September 1972"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal Events"] "\n" [:td {} "29"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Venues"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/543"} "Schwimmhalle, Olympiapark, München"]] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "The 1972 swimming events were held at the Schwimmhalle in München, which was built specifically for the 1972 Olympics. It continues to be used as a swim stadium, but its seating capacity has been reduced from the 9,000 available in 1972 to 1,500. The swim program had been greatly expanded at the past few Olympics, but there was no change in 1972, with the same 15 men’s events and 14 women’s events as in 1968."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The United States was dominant again in 1972, but a bit less so than in 1968, winning 17 of 29 gold medals and 43 of 87 medals. Their men were especially dominant, winning 9 of 15 golds and 26 of 45 medals. Their women won 8 of 14 golds and 17 of 42 medals, but Australian women also won 5 golds."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Two swimmers were the stars of the 1972 Olympics. American " [:a {:href "/athletes/51572"} "Mark Spitz"] " set a goal to win seven gold medals in seven events, and he did so, also winning all of his events in world record time. His seven medals tied the Olympic mark in any sport for most medals at a single Olympics (held by five men), but his seven gold medals at one Games was a new record, broken only in 2008 by " [:a {:href "/athletes/93860"} "Michael Phelps"] ", who won eight gold medals in eight events."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/45050"} "Shane Gould"] " was so heralded coming into München that the Americans wore tee-shirts proclaiming “All That Glitters Is Not Gould.” At the beginning of 1972 Gould held world records at every freestyle distance from 100 to 1,500 metres, but some observers felt that her performances were slightly disappointing in München although she did win three gold medals and five medals."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Events"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n" [:thead {} "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Event"] "\n" [:th {} "Status"] "\n" [:th {} "Date"] "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:th {} "NOCs"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/6172"} "100 metres Freestyle, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "2 –  3 September 1972"] "\n" [:td {} "48"] "\n" [:td {} "29"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/6185"} "200 metres Freestyle, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "29 Au